    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d651">

                <pb facs="0714" n="702" sameAs="frus:frus1943v05/pg702"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">561.333D3/1606: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-12-07T18:00:00-03:00">December 7, 1943—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5622. Private sources report that 8600 bags and 25,600 bags of new crop coffee
                    (reference Embassy’s telegram No. 5596, December 4, 3 p.m.)<anchor n="98"
                        xml:id="d651-f1"/> arrived at Santos on December 6 and December 7
                    respectively, as against Guedes’ recent assurances to the Minister of Finance
                    and Embassy that entries would total 40,000 bags daily.</p>
                <p>Reports from Santos indicate that sales of new crop coffee for shipment to the
                    United States are very quiet due to the rumors that American importers will
                    receive a subsidy of 5 cents per pound and that they will be permitted to
                    increase prices correspondingly.</p>
                <p>Have importers in the United States noticed any improvement in sales or prices of
                    Brazil’s new crop coffee?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d651-f1" n="98" xml:id="d651-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it indicated that there was sufficient coffee on hand at Santos to
                    fill the tonnage assigned (561.333D3/1605).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
